#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111919 :tutorial/content "Many quadratic equations have two solutions. However, it's also possible for a quadratic equation to have only one solution or no real solutions at all.\n\nFor example, consider the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n(3x-6)^2=0\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(3x-6)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3x-6| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormally, at this stage, we would obtain two solutions by setting the inside of the absolute value equal to positive or negative $(\\pm)$ the right-hand side. However, in this case, the right-hand side is zero, and $+0$ and $-0$ are both just $0.$ \n\nSo, we have only one solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x-6| & =0 \\\\ 3x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b3899a9-dbc6-524a-b493-c57a45bfeda9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9203 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111920 :tutorial/content "Let's try and solve the equation $(x+1)^2=-1$ using the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{-1} \\\\ |x+1| & =\\sqrt{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a problem here. We cannot take the square root of a negative number. For that reason, the equation above has *no real solutions*." :tutorial/id #uuid "414b731c-3f76-57ad-985a-3d64bda214e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9205 :tutorial/title "Perfect Square Quadratic Equations With No Real Solutions"}]}
